---
/**
 * foodcraft / AboutBL — "Kalinaw Coffee" (BL), the OUR STORY section.
 *
 * ROLE: the café talking about itself. The design draws a two-column split with
 * the PHOTOGRAPH ON THE LEFT (a tall 540px plate) and the story on the right —
 * eyebrow, Fraunces headline with its italic rust emphasis, the owner's
 * paragraphs, then a two-up hairline SPEC LEDGER of label/value facts.
 *
 * THE DESIGN IS THE SPECIFICATION. Every number below is lifted verbatim from
 * the element carrying data-component="ABOUT" in "Kalinaw Coffee Landing.dc.html":
 *   grid        minmax(0,.92fr) minmax(0,1.08fr), gap 60px, align-items center
 *   photo       LEFT column · height 540px · radius 14px · ground #F2E8D8
 *   eyebrow     10px / .42em / uppercase / 600 / #B4541F   → the wrapper's .kick
 *   headline    Fraunces 400 clamp(31px,4vw,54px)/1.08, margin .26em 0 .34em,
 *               #241A12, with an italic #B4541F <em>       → the wrapper's .h2
 *   paragraphs  16.5px / 1.85 / #6B5C4E / max-width 52ch / margin 0 0 1em,
 *               the LAST one 0 0 28px (the gap above the ledger)
 *   ledger      repeat(2, minmax(0,1fr)), gap 0 32px
 *   ledger row  flex · baseline · gap 12px · padding 12px 0 · border-bottom
 *               1px #EDE2D1                                 → the wrapper's .rowrule
 *   label       14.5px #241A12   ·   value  margin-left:auto 13px #948373
 * The photo stays on the LEFT and the ledger keeps both of its tracks. Neither
 * has been moved to suit an editor mechanism, and no colour has been re-derived
 * to survive a scheme — that pair of habits is what shipped an earlier template
 * looking like a different design.
 *
 * R1 · NO LITERAL HEX. Every hex the design writes here already has a name in
 * PageBL and is used through it:
 *   #F2E8D8 --well · #B4541F --accent · #241A12 --ink · #6B5C4E --ink-soft
 *   #EDE2D1 --line-2 · #948373 --muted · 14px --r-md
 * .plate supplies --well and --r-md, .rowrule supplies the --line-2 hairline and
 * the whole row geometry, .kick supplies the eyebrow and .h2 the headline — so
 * the only values restated below are the ones no primitive knows: the two grid
 * tracks, the 540px plate, the paragraph's 16.5/1.85/52ch, the 28px gap and the
 * two ledger cell types.
 *
 * EDITABLE HOOKS (each one DECLARED in components/editor/genericContentSchema.ts,
 * so every data-field below is a hook a real sidebar input owns):
 *   about.image             (data-image-field) — the left photograph
 *   about.tag               — eyebrow (.kick)
 *   about.headline          — h2, set:html so an owner's own <em> lands wherever
 *                             THEY put it in THEIR sentence; the design's
 *                             signature italic rust is the wrapper's .h2 em rule
 *   about.lead              — the FIRST paragraph. Drawn on purpose: the
 *                             sidebar's "Lead paragraph" box writes about.lead,
 *                             and a schema field no component renders is the
 *                             mirror image of a dead hook and just as broken.
 *   about.paragraphs.${i}   — the remaining paragraphs (set:html)
 *   about.specs.${i}.label  — the ledger's left cell
 *   about.specs.${i}.value  — the ledger's right cell
 * about.signature is READ once — as the photograph's accessible name — and is
 * never printed. See R9 below.
 *
 * R2 · NOTHING VISIBLE IS SUPPLIED BY THIS FILE. Not one word. The design's demo
 * copy ("Our story", "One roaster, six farms, no rush.", the six sample spec
 * rows) is the DESIGNER filling their own template, and the image slot's "Drop
 * the roaster or counter" is an editor affordance, not page copy — none of it
 * ships. A café that wrote no spec rows advertises none.
 * Every guard sits OUTSIDE its tag, so an empty value removes the ELEMENT and
 * not just its text: no empty eyebrow, no headline over nothing, no empty plate,
 * and — because a .rowrule IS its own hairline — no rule drawn under a spec row
 * that is not there.
 * NO STAR ROW exists in this section: the design's ABOUT carries no rating and
 * the schema exposes no numeric rating on about.*, so there is nothing here that
 * could print five stars for a customer who gave no number.
 *
 * DERIVED (computed here, never invented):
 *   · paraRows / specRows drop blank entries but carry the ORIGINAL array index
 *     into data-field (R6), so deleting row 2 cannot misdirect an edit into row 3.
 *   · a spec row survives on EITHER cell — a label the owner typed but has not
 *     filled in yet is still their line; only a wholly empty row is dropped.
 *   · about.paragraphs / about.specs are read tolerantly (array, bare string, or
 *     objects vs bare strings). Draft content has arrived as both shapes before,
 *     and a `.map is not a function` costs the whole page, not one row.
 *   · the grid drops to ONE track whenever only one column has content, so a
 *     missing photo never leaves a 540px hole beside the copy. Above 900px the
 *     surviving column then keeps the MEASURE the design gives it — 637px of
 *     copy, 543px of photo, which ARE the design's two tracks at PageBL's 1240px
 *     .wrap (1240 − 60 gap, split .92fr/1.08fr) — rather than stretching across
 *     the page. This is the common case, not an edge one: derive-content-defaults
 *     seeds no about.image, so a copy-only ABOUT is what a first build renders,
 *     and a ledger free to span 1240px would set each label and its value at
 *     opposite ends of the page instead of drawing the design's ruled ledger.
 *   · the image url has its apostrophes percent-escaped before it goes into
 *     url('…'), so an upload named "kalinaw's roaster.jpg" cannot close the
 *     quote early and kill the whole declaration.
 *
 * SECTION GATE (R5): tag and headline are deliberately NOT counted.
 * lib/derive-content-defaults.ts sets about.tag = 'About' on EVERY site and
 * about.headline = `About ${name}` on every named one, so a gate that counted
 * them would be always-true and would ship a heading over an empty section. The
 * gate counts BODY content only: the lead, the surviving paragraphs, the
 * surviving spec rows, the photograph. (about.lead is also derived, but from the
 * owner's OWN facts — their name, city and business type — so it is a real
 * sentence about a real business, which is what body content means here.)
 *
 * DELIBERATELY NOT RENDERED, so none of these reads as an oversight:
 *   · NO onPage() ANCHOR HELPER (R7). This section renders no href of any kind —
 *     the design's ABOUT has no button, no link and no action, so there is no
 *     data-href-field to remap and a helper that is never called is dead code
 *     that later drifts out of step with the wrapper. If a link is ever added
 *     here it must arrive WITH the helper copied in from a sibling, with
 *     PAGE_ANCHORS = menu/about/gallery/location/faq/main and ANCHOR_ALIAS
 *     mapping visit+contact → location, services+rooms → menu, host+why →
 *     about, work → gallery, book → main. The anchor duty this file DOES own is
 *     its id="about" — one of PageBL's six page anchors, and the target the
 *     header nav and the host/why aliases resolve to.
 *   · about.signature and about.role as VISIBLE text. They belong to a host
 *     identity row, which is a different design; the ABOUT element here draws a
 *     photograph, a story and a spec ledger, and no person.
 *   · a .cap pill on the plate. The design's about photograph carries no caption
 *     and no schema field would let an owner write one, so a caption here could
 *     only be a literal this file invented.
 *   · band-dark. The design draws ABOUT on the plain cream ground; the espresso
 *     bands in this template are HOW-IT-WORKS and CTA-BAND.
 *   · a max-width on the .h2. The wrapper's 24ch cap at the clamp's 54px is
 *     ~648px, which is the design's own 637px column to within a character —
 *     the design constrains this headline with the COLUMN, not with a rule of
 *     its own, so forking the shared primitive would buy nothing.
 * SECTION PADDING is the wrapper's, not this file's. The design writes
 * `padding:96px 32px 0`: the 1240/gutter half is the shared .wrap, and the 96px
 * top is `html[data-page="BL"] section`'s clamp(56px,7.6vw,96px), which caps at
 * exactly that. The design's bottom-0 is a WHOLE-PAGE rhythm decision (every
 * unbanded section in it pads top only) and belongs in the wrapper's shared
 * rule; one section zeroing its own bottom while fourteen siblings keep theirs
 * is exactly the local re-derivation that put an earlier template out of step
 * with its design. Flagged, not done here.
 *
 * R9 · the plate renders ONLY when there is a real upload, so role="img" is
 * honest by construction. Its accessible name is about.signature — the owner's
 * OWN words (derive-content-defaults seeds it from the business name, so on a
 * café the photograph announces as the café). Never a literal written here. With
 * no signature the photo is HIDDEN from the accessibility tree rather than
 * announced as something we guessed, and that is done with ONE conditional
 * aria-hidden — never a literal aria-hidden="true" sitting beside a conditional
 * one, which is the duplicated-attribute bug two BK components shipped: the
 * literal wins and hides content that should have been announced.
 *
 * R3 · one scoped <style>, no is:global. Astro's scoped styles cannot reach
 * elements created by set:html, so the owner's own <em> inside the headline is
 * styled by PageBL's global `:where(.h2 em)` rule (italic, weight 500, rust) and
 * NOT from here. Anything this file ever needs to say about an owner <em> would
 * have to go through :global().
 *
 * R4 · this section has no buttons at all, and none of the engine-pinned class
 * names (.btn-primary .btn-ghost .nav-cta .cta-band .trust .testi, or `.hero` on
 * a <section>) appears anywhere below.
 *
 * CONTRAST, the design's own values: label --ink on --paper 14.6:1, paragraphs
 * --ink-soft on --paper 6.71:1, eyebrow --accent on --paper 5.31:1. The ledger's
 * VALUE cell is --muted at 3.86:1 — below AA, and PageBL's frontmatter already
 * records that this is the design's palette and the owner's call to change in
 * one line. It is not re-derived here; a section quietly darkening a shared
 * token would put this ledger out of step with the three other places --muted is
 * used. Flagged for the owner, unchanged.
 */
// `layout` is accepted because PageBL passes it to every section uniformly;
// this block reads nothing outside content.*.
interface Props { content: any; layout?: any; }
const { content } = Astro.props;
const about = content.about || {};

const tag = about.tag || '';
const headline = about.headline || '';
const lead = about.lead || '';
const image = String(about.image || '');
// An apostrophe in an upload's filename would otherwise close the url('') early
// and take the whole background-image declaration down with it.
const imageCss = image.replace(/'/g, '%27');

// Read tolerantly: a draft has arrived with this as a bare string before, and a
// `.map is not a function` costs the whole page rather than one paragraph. The
// only fallback taken is the SAME path as a string — folding in a legacy
// `about.body` is not done here, because the data-field would then point at
// about.paragraphs.0 while the text lived somewhere else, and an input that
// edits a different string than the one under it is a broken hook.
const paragraphSource: any[] = Array.isArray(about.paragraphs)
  ? about.paragraphs
  : (typeof about.paragraphs === 'string' && about.paragraphs.trim()
    ? [about.paragraphs]
    : []);
// A blank row in the admin's paragraph list would otherwise render an empty <p>
// that still spends its margin. Drop the blanks, carry the ORIGINAL index (R6).
const paraRows = paragraphSource
  .map((text: any, i: number) => ({ text: String(text ?? ''), i }))
  .filter((row) => row.text.trim().length > 0);

// Same discipline for the ledger. An item may legitimately be an object
// {label,value} (what the schema's newItem creates) or, from an older draft, a
// bare string — treated as a label with no value rather than dropped.
const specSource: any[] = Array.isArray(about.specs) ? about.specs : [];
const specRows = specSource
  .map((row: any, i: number) => {
    const item = row && typeof row === 'object' ? row : { label: row, value: '' };
    return {
      label: String(item.label ?? '').trim(),
      value: String(item.value ?? '').trim(),
      i,
    };
  })
  // EITHER cell keeps the row. Only a wholly empty row goes, so no hairline is
  // ever drawn under a row that is not there.
  .filter((row) => row.label.length > 0 || row.value.length > 0);

// The photograph's accessible name — the owner's own words, never a literal.
const photoName = String(about.signature || '').trim();

// Track count, computed here so the published grid never keeps an empty 540px
// column standing beside a single populated one.
const hasCopy = Boolean(tag || headline || lead) || paraRows.length > 0 || specRows.length > 0;
const hasPhoto = Boolean(image);
// R5 — BODY content only. tag and headline are excluded: both are derived onto
// every site, so counting them would make this gate always-true.
const hasSection = Boolean(lead || image) || paraRows.length > 0 || specRows.length > 0;
---
{hasSection && (
  <section class="story" id="about">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class:list={['story-grid', !(hasCopy && hasPhoto) && 'story-grid--solo']}>
        {hasPhoto && (
          /* LEFT column, as the design draws it. .plate already gives the --well
             ground, the --r-md (14px) corner and the overflow clip. */
          <div class="plate story-plate reveal">
            <div
              class="ph"
              data-image-field="about.image"
              role={photoName ? 'img' : undefined}
              aria-label={photoName || undefined}
              aria-hidden={photoName ? undefined : 'true'}
              style={`background-image:url('${imageCss}');`}
            ></div>
          </div>
        )}
        {hasCopy && (
          <div class="story-copy">
            {tag && <span class="kick" data-field="about.tag">{tag}</span>}
            {headline && <h2 class="h2 story-h reveal" data-field="about.headline" set:html={headline}></h2>}
            {/* about.lead comes from a plain textarea, so it is bound as TEXT.
                The paragraph LIST below is where an owner writes markup. */}
            {lead && <p class="lede story-p reveal" data-delay="1" data-field="about.lead">{lead}</p>}
            {paraRows.map((row, k: number) => (
              <p
                class="lede story-p reveal"
                data-delay={String((k % 3) + 1)}
                data-field={`about.paragraphs.${row.i}`}
                set:html={row.text}
              ></p>
            ))}
            {specRows.length > 0 && (
              <div class="story-specs reveal" data-delay="2">
                {specRows.map((row) => (
                  /* .rowrule is the wrapper's shared label/value hairline — the
                     design's spec row exactly. Each cell is guarded on its own,
                     so a half-filled row prints one cell and not one cell plus a
                     gap. */
                  <div class="rowrule story-spec">
                    {row.label && (
                      <span class="story-spec-l" data-field={`about.specs.${row.i}.label`}>{row.label}</span>
                    )}
                    {row.value && (
                      <span class="story-spec-v" data-field={`about.specs.${row.i}.value`}>{row.value}</span>
                    )}
                  </div>
                ))}
              </div>
            )}
          </div>
        )}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
)}
<style>
  /* THE DESIGN'S SPLIT, verbatim: .92fr of photograph against 1.08fr of story,
     60px apart, the two columns centred on each other. The photo leads because
     the design puts it first; that order is also the DOM order, so the single
     column below 900px reads photograph-then-story exactly as the design does. */
  .story-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, .92fr) minmax(0, 1.08fr);
    gap: 60px;
    align-items: center;
  }
  .story-grid--solo { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
  .story-copy { min-width: 0; }

  /* The design has no one-column ABOUT, so --solo is a state this file has to
     answer for. The family answer is to keep the surviving column at the MEASURE
     it has in the design rather than let it stretch across the whole wrap. It
     matters most here: a copy-only ABOUT is the DEFAULT first build —
     derive-content-defaults seeds no about.image — and a ledger free to span
     1240px prints a label and its value at opposite ends of the page, which is
     not the ruled ledger the design draws. 637px and 543px ARE the design's two
     tracks at PageBL's 1240px .wrap (1240 − 60px gap, split 1.08fr / .92fr), so
     this restores the design's proportions rather than inventing new ones. Below
     900px one track is everybody's layout and the caps lift. */
  @media (min-width: 901px) {
    .story-grid--solo .story-copy { max-width: 637px; }
    .story-grid--solo .story-plate { max-width: 543px; }
  }

  /* The eyebrow IS the wrapper's .kick (10px caps, .42em, --accent) — the
     design's span, already named, so nothing about it is restated here and it
     cannot fork from the rest of the template. The one thing ADDED is a wrap
     rule: .kick is an inline-block carrying .42em of tracking, and one long
     owner word (a hyphenless place name) would push the document sideways at
     360px (R8). Only properties the wrapper never sets are added. */
  .story-copy .kick { max-width: 100%; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }

  /* The headline is the wrapper's .h2 (Fraunces 400, clamp(31px,4vw,54px)/1.08,
     --ink); only the design's own margins are set here. The italic rust <em> is
     PageBL's global rule — a scoped rule could not reach markup created by
     set:html (R3). Every rule below is qualified with .story-copy so it
     out-specifies the wrapper's :where() primitives whatever order the sheets
     land in. */
  .story-copy .story-h { margin: .26em 0 .34em; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }

  /* THE PARAGRAPH. The wrapper's .lede already carries the family and the
     design's --ink-soft; the three metrics the design states for THIS section —
     16.5px, 1.85, 52ch — are not .lede's defaults (15.5–17.5px / 1.72 / 44ch),
     so they are set here rather than by bending the shared primitive that eleven
     other sections also use. PageBL's `:where(p){margin:0 0 1em}` is (0,1,1) and
     would beat a bare .lede, hence the parent-class qualifier. */
  .story-copy .story-p {
    font-size: 16.5px;
    line-height: 1.85;
    max-width: 52ch;
    margin: 0 0 1em;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  /* The design gives its LAST paragraph `margin:0 0 28px` — the gap above the
     ledger. That gap is spent by .story-specs below instead, so the same 28px
     appears in the same place while a section with NO ledger ends flush rather
     than trailing 28px of nothing. */
  .story-copy .story-p:last-of-type { margin-bottom: 0; }
  /* A cleared headline is a real case, and then the eyebrow would sit flush on
     the first paragraph — the h2's .26em top margin is what normally opens that
     gap. Adjacency, so it fires only in that case, and qualified because the
     shorthand above would otherwise re-zero the top margin. */
  .story-copy .kick + .story-p { margin-top: 14px; }

  /* THE SPEC LEDGER — two equal columns, 32px apart, no row gap: each row
     carries its own 12px padding and its own hairline (the wrapper's .rowrule),
     which is what makes a continuous ruled ledger rather than a stack of
     separated cards. */
  .story-specs {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 0 32px;
    margin-top: 28px;
  }
  /* .rowrule supplies flex / baseline / 12px gap / 12px 0 padding / the 1px
     --line-2 underline / min-width:0 — the design's row exactly. Only the two
     cell types are stated here. */
  .story-copy .story-spec-l {
    font-size: 14.5px; color: var(--ink);
    min-width: 0; overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  /* `text-align: right` is the ONE property here the design does not write, and
     it is a no-op in the state the design shows: margin-left:auto already puts a
     single-line value hard against the right edge. It takes effect only when a
     value WRAPS — at 520px, or on a long owner value — where the design's own
     flex row would leave the second line ragged-left under a right-aligned
     first. That is the design's alignment continued into a state it never had to
     draw, not a new alignment. */
  .story-copy .story-spec-v {
    margin-left: auto; text-align: right;
    font-size: 13px; color: var(--muted);
    min-width: 0; overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* The photograph's own height. .plate knows the ground, the corner and the
     clip; the design's fixed 540px is the one thing it does not. */
  .story-plate { height: 540px; }

  /* ── R8 · ONE COLUMN BEFORE THE COPY MEASURE IS SQUEEZED. At 900px the .92fr
        track is ~390px, which is about where a 540px photograph stops earning
        its width and the 52ch paragraph starts breaking badly. */
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .story-grid {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
      gap: clamp(28px, 4.4vw, 44px);
    }
    /* Full-bleed at one column, 540px would be most of a laptop viewport. */
    .story-plate { height: clamp(300px, 52vw, 460px); }
  }
  /* PageBL's shared 760 pass releases `.lede { max-width: none }`, but the rule
     above is more specific and would silently defeat it, leaving a 52ch column
     of text inside a full-width phone-landscape wrap. The release is restated
     here so this section follows the wrapper instead of overriding it. */
  @media (max-width: 760px) {
    .story-copy .story-p { max-width: none; }
    .story-specs { gap: 0 24px; }
  }
  /* ── 520: nothing may push the page sideways. The ledger goes to a single
        column (two label/value pairs in ~150px each would break every value onto
        its own line), and both cells already wrap anywhere, so a long unbroken
        string — a URL, an origin name, a hyphenless spec — wraps instead of
        widening the document. There is no button in this section, so the
        full-width white-space rule has nothing to apply to. */
  @media (max-width: 520px) {
    .story-specs { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); margin-top: 24px; }
    .story-plate { height: clamp(240px, 62vw, 340px); }
  }
</style>
